PRIME SUSPECT: THE UMBILICAL, unsupported at the time of measurement
(project.txt item 11 — anchor not built, Key-Bak Sidekick not yet arrived). The
bundle carries the CPAP cable, BOTH water lines and the PTFE tube; it drapes to
one side and its restoring force on the toolhead necessarily varies with X.
That fits every feature: acts in Y, position-dependent and monotonic, broadband
and low-frequency rather than a discrete mode, and it appeared only after the
toolhead bundle was completed.

Note the known geometry fault is on the RIGHT rail — the OPPOSITE end from the
noisy one. No correlation, which is another reason not to treat this as a frame
fault.

RETEST WHEN THE KEY-BAK MOUNT IS BUILT: re-run macros/is_xpos.g unchanged and
compare against 0.366 / 0.495 / 0.228. If left rms falls toward 0.23 it was the
bundle and it is solved. If it stays near 0.35 it is structural and the left
tower and belt path deserve a proper look. Make the bundle geometry REPEATABLE
or run-to-run scatter will swamp the comparison.

